---
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/CallToAction.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import acreagePhoto from '../../assets/shared/kansas/kansas-land-aerial.png';
import prairiePhoto from '../../assets/shared/kansas/kansas-land-prairie.jpg';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Land and Acreage in South-Central Kansas | Charity Menefee"
  description="Grounded real estate guidance for people considering land and acreage around Wichita and south-central Kansas."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Land & acreage"
    title="More room comes with more questions."
    intro="If you're considering rural property, I can help you organize the practical considerations, explore possibilities, and identify which facts need verification."
    image={acreagePhoto}
    imageAlt="Kansas acreage seen from above, with fields, trees, homes, and rural roads"
  />

  <section class="section-space" aria-labelledby="acreage-title">
    <div class="shell editorial-grid">
      <div data-reveal="left">
        <p class="eyebrow">A personal point of reference</p>
        <h2 id="acreage-title">I understand why the details matter.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose" data-reveal="right">
        <p>My own purchase of approximately 10 acres gave me firsthand experience with the additional practical considerations that can come with more land. It taught me to slow down, look beyond the house, and ask which property-specific facts still need answers.</p>
        <p>That experience informs the questions I bring to the conversation, but I don't present it as a specialized credential. Surveyors, title professionals, attorneys, inspectors, utility providers, and other qualified specialists may need to address subjects within their expertise.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="land-feature" aria-labelledby="land-use-title">
    <Picture
      class="land-feature-image"
      src={prairiePhoto}
      formats={['avif', 'webp']}
      widths={[640, 960, 1440, 1920]}
      sizes="100vw"
      alt="Kansas prairie and open land at sunset"
    />
    <div class="shell land-feature-inner">
      <div class="land-feature-card" data-reveal="layer-right">
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">Begin with intended use</p>
        <h2 id="land-use-title">Start with what you want the property to support.</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Live there as it is, or build and add structures later?</li>
          <li>Keep animals, use the land recreationally, or simply have more space?</li>
          <li>How much acreage and ongoing maintenance feel manageable?</li>
          <li>Which uses or improvements need approval or professional review?</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="fine-print">Intended uses and building plans should be verified for the specific property with the appropriate authorities and professionals.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-space" aria-labelledby="land-access-title">
    <div class="shell editorial-grid">
      <div data-reveal="left">
        <p class="eyebrow">Access and utilities</p>
        <h2 id="land-access-title">Availability should be confirmed, not assumed.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose" data-reveal="right">
        <p>Road type, legal and physical access, driveway placement, and recorded easements can affect how a property functions. Marketing remarks aren't a substitute for title information or legal interpretation, so title or legal professionals may need to clarify access rights and recorded documents.</p>
        <p>Utility questions can include electricity, natural gas or propane, the water source, septic or sewer service, and internet availability. The important step is confirming what reaches the property, where connections are located, and what it may take to serve your intended homesite or improvements.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-space section-surface" aria-labelledby="land-water-title">
    <div class="shell editorial-grid">
      <div data-reveal="left">
        <p class="eyebrow">Water and wastewater</p>
        <h2 id="land-water-title">Well and septic systems bring their own due diligence.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose" data-reveal="right">
        <p>A property served by a well or septic system raises different questions than one connected to city services. Buyers may need to learn the location, condition, capacity, maintenance history, and suitability of existing systems or a proposed site.</p>
        <p>Those answers should come from qualified inspectors, contractors, local authorities, or other appropriate specialists. I can help identify which information is missing and keep those evaluations connected to the larger purchase.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-space section-sage" aria-labelledby="land-boundaries-title">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading split-heading">
        <div data-reveal="left">
          <p class="eyebrow">Boundaries and use</p>
          <h2 id="land-boundaries-title">The listing description is a starting point.</h2>
        </div>
        <p data-reveal="right">Acreage, boundaries, restrictions, and permitted uses deserve property-specific verification before they become part of your plan.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-list reveal-stagger">
        <article data-reveal="up">
          <span>Boundaries</span>
          <h3>Know what is being conveyed</h3>
          <p>Marketing acreage shouldn't be treated as a survey. Recorded information, title work, and a survey when appropriate can help establish the legal description and physical boundaries.</p>
        </article>
        <article data-reveal="up">
          <span>Restrictions</span>
          <h3>Check the intended use</h3>
          <p>Zoning, deed restrictions, covenants, association rules, and building requirements can vary by property. Plans for animals, additional structures, or future construction need direct verification.</p>
        </article>
        <article data-reveal="up">
          <span>Site conditions</span>
          <h3>Account for the whole property</h3>
          <p>Outbuildings, fences, driveways, drainage, ponds, trees, terrain, and maintenance can matter as much as the house. Qualified professionals should evaluate condition or technical concerns.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-space" aria-labelledby="land-purchase-title">
    <div class="shell editorial-grid">
      <div data-reveal="left">
        <p class="eyebrow">Bringing it together</p>
        <h2 id="land-purchase-title">Acreage isn't simply a house with a bigger yard.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose" data-reveal="right">
        <p>The home, land, access, services, improvements, and future plans all need to make sense together. Some answers may be simple, while others require records, estimates, inspections, or professional advice before you can judge the property clearly.</p>
        <p>If the property includes a home, the broader <a href="/services/buying/">home-buying process</a> still applies. I'll help keep the additional acreage items visible so an appealing view or extra space doesn't push important facts into the background.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CallToAction
    title="Have questions about a particular acreage property?"
    copy="Send me the listing or tell me what you're hoping the land can support. I'll help you organize the access, utility, and property-specific items that need a closer look."
  />
</BaseLayout>
